{
  "title": "Shared test vectors for recipe scaling (web + iOS), pilot for the \
vector pattern",
  "description": "Recipe scaling is implemented twice — \
ramekin-ui/src/utils/scaleAmount.ts and \
ramekin-ios/Ramekin/RecipeScaleSupport.swift — and has already drifted: the \
Swift version accepts locale decimals (\"1,5\") and formats results with a \
locale-aware NumberFormatter, while the TS version only accepts and emits \
\".\" decimals. The same amount can scale to different strings per platform.\n\
\n\
Per doc/client-logic-sharing.md, pin this with shared test vectors — a \
checked-in JSON file of {input amount, factor, expected output} cases consumed \
by both the Vitest suite and an XCTest. Divergence then fails CI instead of \
being discovered by users. This is the pilot for the pattern, so it also \
establishes the conventions the other areas will reuse:\n\
\n\
- Vector file location: shared-test-vectors/ at the repo root (e.g. \
shared-test-vectors/scale-amount.json), with a small README describing the \
format.\n\
- TS consumption: Vitest test that imports/reads the JSON and asserts \
scaleAmount(input, factor) === expected.\n\
- Swift consumption: XCTest loading the JSON as a test resource (xcodegen \
project.yml resources entry pointing at the shared directory), asserting \
RecipeScaleSupport.scaleAmount.\n\
- While writing the vectors, reconcile the known divergences and fix whichever \
side loses: decide whether \",\" decimals parse (recommend yes on both, \
matching Swift), and make output formatting canonical \".\" on both (pin the \
Swift NumberFormatter to a fixed locale rather than the device locale so \
vectors are deterministic).\n\
- CI wiring so one-sided edits actually fail: add shared-test-vectors/ to the \
trigger paths of .github/workflows/ios.yml (today it only fires on \
ramekin-ios/** changes, so a vector edit would merge without running the Swift \
side). The Vitest side runs in main CI already once p3-web-unit-test-runner \
lands.\n\
\n\
Blocked on p3-web-unit-test-runner landing first.",
